          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the 'reasonable efforts' requirement of the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act creates no federal right enforceable by children or parents through § 1983 or an implied right of action; enforcement lies with the Secretary through the state-plan approval process.",
          "relevance": "It locates CFSR-style compliance monitoring in HHS's administrative apparatus rather than in private enforcement actions.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that a state agency's failure to protect a child from violence by his father, even after repeated reports of abuse, did not deprive the child of liberty without due process because the Constitution imposes no general affirmative duty on the State to protect against private violence.",
          "relevance": "It frames why performance monitoring, not constitutional litigation, is the principal check on agency non-performance.",
          "quote": "a State's failure to protect an individual against private violence simply does not constitute a violation of the Due Process Clause",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        },
        {
